# Stack & architecture

## Product model

- Exactly two roles: **coach** and **client** (`app_role`: `coach` | `user`).
- Each client has their own workspace/portal; coaches invite clients by email.
- Core goal: automate admin so coaches focus on clients.

## Coach-facing pages

Dashboard · Clients · Workouts · Nutrition templates · Tasks · Configuration (`/settings`)

## Client-facing pages

Dashboard · Training · Nutrition · Progression

## Stack (do not reinvent)

| Layer | Choice |
| --- | --- |
| App | Vite + React + TypeScript + React Router |
| UI | Untitled UI (primary); legacy shadcn under migration |
| Data | Neon Postgres (`DATABASE_URL`, pooled) |
| Auth | Neon Auth (Managed Better Auth) |
| API | Vercel serverless functions under `api/` |
| Files | Vercel Blob |
| Hosting | Vercel (`vercel.json` already configured) |

## Data access conventions

- Frontend table access goes through `db.from(...)` (`src/lib/db.ts`), not raw Supabase clients for new code.
- Server handlers use `withUser` / RLS impersonation (`api/_lib/rls.ts`) or `withService` for trusted jobs.
- Never commit secrets (`.env.local`, Neon API keys, DB passwords). Use `.env.example` as the template.
- Neon project for this foundation: `coaching-platform` (`fragrant-queen-24129188`, `aws-eu-central-1`). Production cutover env vars live in Vercel — do not hardcode connection strings.

## Automation bias

When adding features, prefer workflows that remove repetitive coach admin (templates, scheduling, reminders, invites) over one-off manual screens.
